  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery early this afternoon shows an upper low spinning \nover eastern Nebraska/western Iowa with ridging on both sides of it. \nAt the surface, a cold/stationary front is draped from the Mid-\nAtlantic states westward into Arkansas with high pressure centered \nover Ontario. Widespread cloud cover will remain in place through at \nleast Tuesday morning as low-level moisture remains trapped beneath \na temperature inversion, holding temperatures several degrees cooler \nthan normal. \n\nSpotty showers/drizzle are noted on regional radar within the \nvicinity of the aforementioned upper low from central Iowa into \nnorthwest Illinois, and also downwind of Lake Michigan where steady \nnortheast winds are spreading moisture inland. Similar conditions \nare expected to expand east through Tuesday morning as the upper low \npositions itself over eastern Iowa/northwest Illinois by daybreak. \nThese should be very light in nature and amount to little \naccumulation. Temperatures on Tuesday will be similar to today due \nto widespread cloud cover/precipitation with overnight lows \ndropping into the low to middle 50s. Subsidence on the backside \nof the upper low should result in some pockets of clearing \nacross west- central Illinois Tuesday night, potentially opening\nthe door for a few spots to dip down into the upper 40s (20-40%\nchance).\n\nBy midweek, the upper low meanders into the Mid-Atlantic states with \nsubtle ridging building in its wake. Temperatures will remain cool \nthrough the end of the week, then gradually warm back into the 70s \nto low 80s by the latter half of the weekend. Precipitation chances \nlook minimal midweek through the weekend, though a developing low \nover the Plains states may begin to encroach on the Midwest states \nlater this weekend.",
